   "answer": "Harrow is not a Region B covered provider: it is a private clinic that does not bill a Region B health insurer electronically and is not an insurer. The Health Records Act duties in sections 4\u20138 therefore do not apply to it, and it is not a service agent because it keeps the referral in its own clinical record for its own care rather than on behalf of a covered provider. The patient's record-access right under section 10 likewise applies only to records maintained by a covered provider, so section 10 does not impose an access-response duty on Harrow. Harrow is also not a data host on the supplied facts: it stores the referral for its own care and not on behalf of another person. Its stated clinical retention schedule therefore is not a Region B section 9 data-host retention duty. The Region B provisions read contain no separate professional-retention obligation applicable to this ordinary non-covered private clinic, and no provision requires Harrow to retain records in a way that would itself prevent compliance with approved transfer clauses under Article 18. Section 13 only authorises the Health Records Commissioner to prescribe forms, agreement content and safeguards; it does not create a professional obligation on Harrow on these facts.\n\nThe Records Access Act gives the Records Access Commissioner a significant public-authority power, but only against a 'computing provider': for national security or serious-crime investigations, the Commissioner may order such a provider, without court authorisation and regardless of the affected person's nationality or residence, to produce records stored by or accessible to it, in Region B or elsewhere. An order may include a renewable non-disclosure direction. The provider need not decrypt records or produce keys it neither holds nor can obtain, and the person concerned has no statutory right to notice or review. Harrow is described as a clinical provider using its own clinical record, not as a person providing storage, hosting or computing services; no current section 3 order or other Records Access Act event is supplied. Accordingly, the existing finding that no present production, access or related event is engaged is unchanged. If Harrow's portal were instead operated as a storage, hosting or computing service supplied by Harrow, section 3 could become relevant and a future order could create a conflict with restrictive transfer clauses; that conditional power is not a present obligation or event on the stated facts. The encrypted upload is a security measure in practice, but it does not activate a Health Records Act duty for Harrow because Harrow is neither a covered provider nor a service agent.",
